## sess118 — the publication half is wired, and the RULE-5 consult came back with a longer list than the landing closed

**Build: VERSION still `0.11.441` (not revved — the landing is incomplete), srcversion `3C6F0BF4547E9A22C95A0ED` → `AC44AC4FAF6EF5DA0C24299`. Builds clean with zero warnings — the two "defined but not used" warnings sess117 left behind were exactly the unwired half, and they are gone. Still STOP-SHIP: fleet stays on 0.11.440.**

**What landed.** `lreq_clr_snap`/`lreq_clr_still_good` are now called from all three memory-only publication arms — the two already-held shortcuts and the direct-handoff adopt arm. I placed the adopt-arm validation as an `else if` ahead of the `else` block so it runs before every side effect rather than after them; that arm had no defence of any kind before this.

**One thing I changed against the written design.** It said to snapshot *after* the slot read. That is wrong: a clear that both begins and commits inside the read→snap gap leaves `clr_active` back at 0 and `clr_seq` already at its new value, so the later validation compares equal and publishes on an image whose holder bit is gone. I armed the snapshot *before* the read instead. GPT confirmed the correction and reproduced the same missed execution independently.

**The consult did not clear the landing.** Two hard blockers plus six conditional. The two that matter most:

- My argument that the residual window between validation and the caller's *use* of the grant is out of scope for `dlm_caw.c` was accepted as technically correct in every particular — and rejected as a scope conclusion. It remains a release blocker with exactly three acceptable dispositions, and RULE 0 buys no exemption: clearing early to keep the peer fast turns a performance failure into corruption.
- The 50ms bounded fail-forward wait I chose in sess117 is not acceptable. "The next attempt collects the owed record" assumes there is a next attempt from this mount; if there isn't, a stranded waiter bit blocks another node for the rest of the mount. The fix GPT prefers is a restructure I hadn't considered — record the owed intent under the registry mutex *before* the long disk-I/O portion, so `lreq_finish` synchronizes with the short intent phase and the wait disappears rather than being bounded.

I landed the two unambiguous items from the checklist while the tree was open: validation now fails closed when the registry entry is missing (was passing), and `tenure[]` saturates instead of wrapping — nothing decrements it in the cached-grant model, so a wrap to zero would have read as "no local tenure" and authorized the exact clear the guard exists to refuse.

Six blockers remain open, with the ambiguous-CAW-result audit and the `mxfs_dlm_caw_unlock_gen` ENOMEM path (it still proceeds unregistered) as the next two. The ledger entry for `D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION` still has not been updated past the sess115 ruling.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, and the DLM core is mid-landing under an active stop-ship ruling. The marker stays unwritten.

